  "blocks": [
    {
      "id": "b-000",
      "tag": "h1",
      "chunk_id": 1,
      "start_sec": 0.0,
      "end_sec": 3.741,
      "words": 6,
      "weight": 9,
      "text": "Chapter 2 — The First Environment",
      "start_ratio": 0.0,
      "end_ratio": 0.0023
    },
    {
      "id": "b-001",
      "tag": "p",
      "chunk_id": 2,
      "start_sec": 3.741,
      "end_sec": 17.732,
      "words": 32,
      "weight": 32,
      "text": "A mother reads the same story aloud, day after day, in late pregnancy. Hours after birth, her newborn sucks in a pattern that brings that story back, and not a different one.",
      "start_ratio": 0.0023,
      "end_ratio": 0.011
    },
    {
      "id": "b-002",
      "tag": "p",
      "chunk_id": 3,
      "start_sec": 17.732,
      "end_sec": 22.803,
      "words": 8,
      "weight": 8,
      "text": "The scene is ordinary. It is also strange.",
      "start_ratio": 0.011,
      "end_ratio": 0.0141
    },
    {
      "id": "b-003",
      "tag": "p",
      "chunk_id": 4,
      "start_sec": 22.803,
      "end_sec": 52.434,
      "words": 67,
      "weight": 67,
      "text": "If the womb were a sealed chamber, the newborn should have no reason to prefer one passage over another. If the fetus were already a little student, the same scene would be taken as proof of prenatal schooling. Neither reading fits what can be shown. Evidence indicates that late-pregnancy speech exposure can shape newborn acoustic preference, but offers no support for lasting cognitive advantage or adult-like learning.",
      "start_ratio": 0.0141,
      "end_ratio": 0.0324
    },
    {
      "id": "b-004",
      "tag": "p",
      "chunk_id": 5,
      "start_sec": 52.434,
      "end_sec": 73.421,
      "words": 29,
      "weight": 29,
      "text": "This chapter asks a narrower question than “does the fetus learn?” It asks what kind of environment pregnancy actually is, and what kinds of claims that environment can support.",
      "start_ratio": 0.0324,
      "end_ratio": 0.0454
    },
    {
      "id": "b-005",
      "tag": "h2",
      "chunk_id": 6,
      "start_sec": 73.421,
      "end_sec": 74.962,
      "words": 2,
      "weight": 4,
      "text": "The puzzle",
      "start_ratio": 0.0454,
      "end_ratio": 0.0464
    },
    {
      "id": "b-006",
      "tag": "p",
      "chunk_id": 7,
      "start_sec": 74.962,
      "end_sec": 90.47,
      "words": 24,
      "weight": 24,
      "text": "The naive picture is a pipeline: the mother’s world pours into the child. The equally naive picture is isolation: nothing important happens until birth.",
      "start_ratio": 0.0464,
      "end_ratio": 0.056
    },
    {
      "id": "b-007",
      "tag": "p",
      "chunk_id": 8,
      "start_sec": 90.47,
      "end_sec": 128.057,
      "words": 49,
      "weight": 49,
      "text": "What we meet instead is a mediated system. External conditions affect maternal physiology. Maternal physiology interacts with the placenta. The placenta transports, metabolizes, restricts, or facilitates. The fetus receives a filtered dose and answers with its own developing physiology. The useful chain is not environment → fetus. It is:",
      "start_ratio": 0.056,
      "end_ratio": 0.0792
    },
    {
      "id": "b-008",
      "tag": "p",
      "chunk_id": 9,
      "start_sec": 128.057,
      "end_sec": 148.205,
      "words": 15,
      "weight": 15,
      "text": "external environment → maternal physiology → placenta → fetal exposure → developmental process → outcome.",
      "start_ratio": 0.0792,
      "end_ratio": 0.0917
    },
    {
      "id": "b-009",
      "tag": "p",
      "chunk_id": 10,
      "start_sec": 148.205,
      "end_sec": 174.552,
      "words": 34,
      "weight": 34,
      "text": "A prenatal exposure is not automatically a fetal dose. A fetal response is not automatically an injury. A developmental difference is not automatically a lasting trait. A population association is not an individual forecast.",
      "start_ratio": 0.0917,
      "end_ratio": 0.108
    },
    {
      "id": "b-010",
      "tag": "p",
      "chunk_id": 11,
      "start_sec": 174.552,
      "end_sec": 188.3,
      "words": 11,
      "weight": 11,
      "text": "Those are not polite disclaimers. They are part of the science.",
      "start_ratio": 0.108,
      "end_ratio": 0.1165
    },
    {
      "id": "b-011",
      "tag": "h2",
      "chunk_id": 12,
      "start_sec": 188.3,
      "end_sec": 191.961,
      "words": 6,
      "weight": 8,
      "text": "Competing explanations of the opening scene",
      "start_ratio": 0.1165,
      "end_ratio": 0.1188
    },
    {
      "id": "b-012",
      "tag": "p",
      "chunk_id": 13,
      "start_sec": 191.961,
      "end_sec": 207.908,
      "words": 12,
      "weight": 12,
      "text": "Before resolving the story-and-voice observation, three explanations need to sit in view:",
      "start_ratio": 0.1188,
      "end_ratio": 0.1286
    },
    {
      "id": "b-013",
      "tag": "li",
      "chunk_id": 14,
      "start_sec": 207.908,
      "end_sec": 223.995,
      "words": 17,
      "weight": 18,
      "text": "Prenatal learning. The fetus encodes the passage as memory, the way a child later encodes a song.",
      "start_ratio": 0.1286,
      "end_ratio": 0.1386
    },
    {
      "id": "b-014",
      "tag": "li",
      "chunk_id": 15,
      "start_sec": 223.995,
      "end_sec": 241.603,
      "words": 12,
      "weight": 13,
      "text": "Perceptual tuning. Repeated sound biases attention and preference without licensing complex cognition.",
      "start_ratio": 0.1386,
      "end_ratio": 0.1495
    },
    {
      "id": "b-015",
      "tag": "li",
      "chunk_id": 16,
      "start_sec": 241.603,
      "end_sec": 258.37,
      "words": 15,
      "weight": 16,
      "text": "Shared postnatal life. The same parent continues talking; preference is maintained or inflated after birth.",
      "start_ratio": 0.1495,
      "end_ratio": 0.1599
    },
    {
      "id": "b-016",
      "tag": "p",
      "chunk_id": 17,
      "start_sec": 258.37,
      "end_sec": 286.038,
      "words": 43,
      "weight": 43,
      "text": "The first is the popular story. The second is the claim the evidence can carry. The third is a standing threat to over-translation. Genetic confounding matters more for stress and IQ than for this particular preference experiment, but over-translation remains the main risk.",
      "start_ratio": 0.1599,
      "end_ratio": 0.177
    },
    {
      "id": "b-017",
      "tag": "h2",
      "chunk_id": 18,
      "start_sec": 286.038,
      "end_sec": 298.255,
      "words": 4,
      "weight": 6,
      "text": "A clock before memory",
      "start_ratio": 0.177,
      "end_ratio": 0.1845
    },
    {
      "id": "b-018",
      "tag": "p",
      "chunk_id": 19,
      "start_sec": 298.255,
      "end_sec": 324.363,
      "words": 26,
      "weight": 26,
      "text": "Human development is sequence, not mere growth. Cells divide and take identities. Tissues fold. Organs appear. Neural cells proliferate and migrate. Later, growth and maturation dominate.",
      "start_ratio": 0.1845,
      "end_ratio": 0.2007
    },
    {
      "id": "b-019",
      "tag": "p",
      "chunk_id": 20,
      "start_sec": 324.363,
      "end_sec": 353.91,
      "words": 37,
      "weight": 37,
      "text": "There is not one timetable. The nervous system, the heart, the placenta, and nutritional demand each have their own. Preimplantation life, gastrulation, organogenesis, and later fetal growth are not interchangeable “stages of pregnancy.” They are different jobs.",
      "start_ratio": 0.2007,
      "end_ratio": 0.219
    },
    {
      "id": "b-020",
      "tag": "p",
      "chunk_id": 21,
      "start_sec": 353.91,
      "end_sec": 395.138,
      "words": 66,
      "weight": 66,
      "text": "That is why timing matters. An exposure during specification of a structure is not the same exposure after the structure exists. Thalidomide remains the classic illustration: an agent with minimal adult toxicity and a devastating, time-locked structural effect. Most prenatal questions are not thalidomide, but the foundational principle of teratology holds: biological process, dose, and tissue accessibility decide outcomes far more than an arbitrary calendar date.",
      "start_ratio": 0.219,
      "end_ratio": 0.2445
    },
    {
      "id": "b-021",
      "tag": "p",
      "chunk_id": 22,
      "start_sec": 395.138,
      "end_sec": 412.765,
      "words": 20,
      "weight": 20,
      "text": "A sensitive window is not an inevitable-injury window. The language of one closing opportunity is too coarse for the biology.",
      "start_ratio": 0.2445,
      "end_ratio": 0.2554
    },
    {
      "id": "b-022",
      "tag": "p",
      "chunk_id": 23,
      "start_sec": 412.765,
      "end_sec": 441.153,
      "words": 44,
      "weight": 44,
      "text": "The better scientific question is always more precise: sensitive to what, at what time, at what dose, through what pathway, producing what outcome? That is the beginning of developmental reasoning, and it is why this book refuses a single “prenatal environment” as a slogan.",
      "start_ratio": 0.2554,
      "end_ratio": 0.273
    },
    {
      "id": "b-023",
      "tag": "h2",
      "chunk_id": 24,
      "start_sec": 441.153,
      "end_sec": 452.09,
      "words": 2,
      "weight": 4,
      "text": "The placenta",
      "start_ratio": 0.273,
      "end_ratio": 0.2797
    },
    {
      "id": "b-024",
      "tag": "p",
      "chunk_id": 25,
      "start_sec": 452.09,
      "end_sec": 468.558,
      "words": 16,
      "weight": 16,
      "text": "If the opening anomaly is a voice, the explanatory engine of the chapter is the placenta.",
      "start_ratio": 0.2797,
      "end_ratio": 0.2899
    },
    {
      "id": "b-025",
      "tag": "p",
      "chunk_id": 26,
      "start_sec": 468.558,
      "end_sec": 501.785,
      "words": 43,
      "weight": 43,
      "text": "It is temporary. It is discarded. During pregnancy it is nonetheless an exchange organ, an endocrine organ, a metabolic regulator, and an immune interface (Burton & Fowden 2015). For a time it does work later distributed across lungs, gut, liver, kidney, and glands.",
      "start_ratio": 0.2899,
      "end_ratio": 0.3105
    },
    {
      "id": "b-026",
      "tag": "p",
      "chunk_id": 27,
      "start_sec": 501.785,
      "end_sec": 539.812,
      "words": 50,
      "weight": 50,
      "text": "“Barrier” is an incomplete metaphor. A barrier only separates. The placenta selects, transports, transforms, buffers, and changes. Different molecules cross by different routes. Some diffuse. Others need transporters. Some are metabolized before they reach fetal blood. Maternal and fetal flow, gradients, surface area, and enzyme expression all decide what arrives.",
      "start_ratio": 0.3105,
      "end_ratio": 0.334
    },
    {
      "id": "b-027",
      "tag": "p",
      "chunk_id": 28,
      "start_sec": 539.812,
      "end_sec": 565.92,
      "words": 31,
      "weight": 31,
      "text": "This is why “the mother was exposed” is biologically incomplete. The harder, better question is: how much of what, in what form, crossed which interface, when, and with what developmental consequence?",
      "start_ratio": 0.334,
      "end_ratio": 0.3502
    },
    {
      "id": "b-028",
      "tag": "p",
      "chunk_id": 29,
      "start_sec": 565.92,
      "end_sec": 606.547,
      "words": 60,
      "weight": 60,
      "text": "One documented buffer is placental 11β-HSD2, which converts much active cortisol to cortisone. The buffer is real, though not absolute (Seckl & Holmes 2007). Experimental disruption of the enzyme in animal models and rare human HSD11B2 mutations associate with reduced birthweight. Yet physiological buffering provides no support for the claim that an ordinary anxious week writes a lifelong psychological outcome.",
      "start_ratio": 0.3502,
      "end_ratio": 0.3753
    },
    {
      "id": "b-029",
      "tag": "p",
      "chunk_id": 30,
      "start_sec": 606.547,
      "end_sec": 621.935,
      "words": 13,
      "weight": 13,
      "text": "A mechanism can be real without supporting the strongest story built around it.",
      "start_ratio": 0.3753,
      "end_ratio": 0.3848
    },
    {
      "id": "b-030",
      "tag": "p",
      "chunk_id": 31,
      "start_sec": 621.935,
      "end_sec": 666.802,
      "words": 66,
      "weight": 66,
      "text": "Fetal circulation makes the same point in another register. Oxygen arrives through the placenta, not the lungs. Temporary shunts (ductus venosus, foramen ovale, ductus arteriosus) route blood for an underwater life. At birth those arrangements must be reorganized. That reorganization is Chapter 3’s territory, not a claim that prenatal wiring writes character. Circulation here is interpretive support for mediation and continuity, not a standalone child-outcome card.",
      "start_ratio": 0.3848,
      "end_ratio": 0.4126
    },
    {
      "id": "b-031",
      "tag": "h2",
      "chunk_id": 32,
      "start_sec": 666.802,
      "end_sec": 677.9,
      "words": 4,
      "weight": 6,
      "text": "Building a brain, unfinished",
      "start_ratio": 0.4126,
      "end_ratio": 0.4195
    },
    {
      "id": "b-032",
      "tag": "p",
      "chunk_id": 33,
      "start_sec": 677.9,
      "end_sec": 718.127,
      "words": 57,
      "weight": 57,
      "text": "Prenatal neural development proceeds through overlapping proliferation, migration, and the rise and fall of transient embryonic zones, including a subplate the classic 1970 Boulder model did not name (Bystron, Blakemore & Rakic 2008). The architectural sequence is established with high anatomical certainty, but offers no basis for treating structural milestones as individual forecasts of intelligence or temperament.",
      "start_ratio": 0.4195,
      "end_ratio": 0.4443
    },
    {
      "id": "b-033",
      "tag": "p",
      "chunk_id": 34,
      "start_sec": 718.127,
      "end_sec": 743.835,
      "words": 33,
      "weight": 33,
      "text": "The newborn brain is neither blank nor finished. Birth does not complete construction. Postnatal experience continues it. Collapse of structure, connectivity, cognition, and mental health into one “brain” claim is the usual error.",
      "start_ratio": 0.4443,
      "end_ratio": 0.4603
    },
    {
      "id": "b-034",
      "tag": "p",
      "chunk_id": 35,
      "start_sec": 743.835,
      "end_sec": 770.102,
      "words": 31,
      "weight": 31,
      "text": "Severe disruptions—extreme growth restriction, certain congenital infections—show that construction can be injured. They are contrast cases. They do not license treating typical pregnancy as a construction site the parent must micromanage.",
      "start_ratio": 0.4603,
      "end_ratio": 0.4765
    },
    {
      "id": "b-035",
      "tag": "h2",
      "chunk_id": 36,
      "start_sec": 770.102,
      "end_sec": 782.8,
      "words": 6,
      "weight": 8,
      "text": "The fetus as a present organism",
      "start_ratio": 0.4765,
      "end_ratio": 0.4844
    },
    {
      "id": "b-036",
      "tag": "p",
      "chunk_id": 37,
      "start_sec": 782.8,
      "end_sec": 820.787,
      "words": 55,
      "weight": 55,
      "text": "In the second half of gestation, fetal heart rate typically falls while variability and movement–heart-rate coupling increase (DiPietro et al. 2015). Across hundreds of maternal–fetal pairs, those trajectories are orderly enough to describe neuromaturation: rank order is somewhat preserved, a transitional period appears near 30 weeks, and growth-restricted fetuses can deviate from the usual path.",
      "start_ratio": 0.4844,
      "end_ratio": 0.5079
    },
    {
      "id": "b-037",
      "tag": "p",
      "chunk_id": 38,
      "start_sec": 820.787,
      "end_sec": 848.014,
      "words": 34,
      "weight": 34,
      "text": "These measures support reliable claims about present autonomic capacity in the womb; they do not provide a temperament passport. Physiological couplings reflect contemporary neuromaturation rather than fixed character traits that travel intact into childhood.",
      "start_ratio": 0.5079,
      "end_ratio": 0.5247
    },
    {
      "id": "b-038",
      "tag": "p",
      "chunk_id": 39,
      "start_sec": 848.014,
      "end_sec": 875.642,
      "words": 43,
      "weight": 43,
      "text": "A quiet fetus is not a quiet child. An active fetus is not an “already-themselves” person. Fetal behavior is interesting because it is happening now. When a measure is used as a crystal ball, it has left Science and entered a different genre.",
      "start_ratio": 0.5247,
      "end_ratio": 0.5418
    },
    {
      "id": "b-039",
      "tag": "h2",
      "chunk_id": 40,
      "start_sec": 875.642,
      "end_sec": 891.339,
      "words": 8,
      "weight": 10,
      "text": "Nutrition as construction material, not a personality tool",
      "start_ratio": 0.5418,
      "end_ratio": 0.5515
    },
    {
      "id": "b-040",
      "tag": "p",
      "chunk_id": 41,
      "start_sec": 891.339,
      "end_sec": 909.367,
      "words": 17,
      "weight": 17,
      "text": "Two nutritional claims in this chapter have unusually clear identification, and they are not the same claim.",
      "start_ratio": 0.5515,
      "end_ratio": 0.5627
    },
    {
      "id": "b-041",
      "tag": "p",
      "chunk_id": 42,
      "start_sec": 909.367,
      "end_sec": 932.874,
      "words": 23,
      "weight": 23,
      "text": "Periconceptional folic acid reduces neural-tube-defect risk by more than two-thirds across randomized trials, providing a profound structural safeguard without altering general cognitive development.",
      "start_ratio": 0.5627,
      "end_ratio": 0.5772
    },
    {
      "id": "b-042",
      "tag": "p",
      "chunk_id": 43,
      "start_sec": 932.874,
      "end_sec": 969.262,
      "words": 46,
      "weight": 46,
      "text": "Severe iodine deficiency in early pregnancy similarly disrupts neurodevelopment because fetal brain thyroid hormone depends, before mid-gestation, on maternal T4 (Zimmermann et al. 2008; Morreale de Escobar et al. 2004). Late correction cannot rewind early corticogenesis, though mild variations in already-replete populations show no comparable injury.",
      "start_ratio": 0.5772,
      "end_ratio": 0.5997
    },
    {
      "id": "b-043",
      "tag": "p",
      "chunk_id": 44,
      "start_sec": 969.262,
      "end_sec": 1010.889,
      "words": 71,
      "weight": 71,
      "text": "Iodine is also the chapter’s clearest timing story. Before the fetal thyroid is a reliable source, the developing cortex depends on maternal T4; T3 generated locally in brain tissue reaches biologically relevant concentrations by mid-gestation. A severe early shortage can injure processes that later iodine will not reconstruct. That is not an argument that every learning difficulty is a missed tablet, or that more is better once a population is replete.",
      "start_ratio": 0.5997,
      "end_ratio": 0.6255
    },
    {
      "id": "b-044",
      "tag": "p",
      "chunk_id": 45,
      "start_sec": 1010.889,
      "end_sec": 1030.197,
      "words": 16,
      "weight": 16,
      "text": "Folate and iodine are not a general argument for supplement maximalism. They are specific substrate-and-timing claims.",
      "start_ratio": 0.6255,
      "end_ratio": 0.6374
    },
    {
      "id": "b-045",
      "tag": "h2",
      "chunk_id": 46,
      "start_sec": 1030.197,
      "end_sec": 1042.774,
      "words": 4,
      "weight": 6,
      "text": "Stress, without the forecast",
      "start_ratio": 0.6374,
      "end_ratio": 0.6452
    },
    {
      "id": "b-046",
      "tag": "p",
      "chunk_id": 47,
      "start_sec": 1042.774,
      "end_sec": 1066.202,
      "words": 29,
      "weight": 29,
      "text": "Observational studies often report that prenatal stress or anxiety goes with smaller babies or later emotional difficulties. The popular translation is simple: a worried pregnancy writes a worried child.",
      "start_ratio": 0.6452,
      "end_ratio": 0.6597
    },
    {
      "id": "b-047",
      "tag": "p",
      "chunk_id": 48,
      "start_sec": 1066.202,
      "end_sec": 1099.309,
      "words": 39,
      "weight": 39,
      "text": "Stronger designs interrupt that translation. When siblings, children-of-twins, and related genetically informed methods are used, the prenatal-specific associations shrink substantially, demonstrating that observational correlations were largely driven by shared family genetics and continuous postnatal conditions rather than intrauterine programming.",
      "start_ratio": 0.6597,
      "end_ratio": 0.6802
    },
    {
      "id": "b-048",
      "tag": "p",
      "chunk_id": 49,
      "start_sec": 1099.309,
      "end_sec": 1132.617,
      "words": 50,
      "weight": 50,
      "text": "Physiology can be real (HPA, placental 11β-HSD2) without licensing a general lifelong forecast. Concurrent postnatal maternal symptoms are a different, still-modest claim. Disaster and clinical trauma are not the default exposure in this card. The chapter’s stress section is a boundary, not a pep talk and not a warning poster.",
      "start_ratio": 0.6802,
      "end_ratio": 0.7008
    },
    {
      "id": "b-049",
      "tag": "h2",
      "chunk_id": 50,
      "start_sec": 1132.617,
      "end_sec": 1145.234,
      "words": 4,
      "weight": 6,
      "text": "Alcohol, tobacco, and mixtures",
      "start_ratio": 0.7008,
      "end_ratio": 0.7086
    },
    {
      "id": "b-050",
      "tag": "p",
      "chunk_id": 51,
      "start_sec": 1145.234,
      "end_sec": 1162.181,
      "words": 16,
      "weight": 16,
      "text": "Not every prenatal exposure is a vague “environment.” Some are named teratogens with different identification strength.",
      "start_ratio": 0.7086,
      "end_ratio": 0.7191
    },
    {
      "id": "b-051",
      "tag": "p",
      "chunk_id": 52,
      "start_sec": 1162.181,
      "end_sec": 1200.649,
      "words": 42,
      "weight": 42,
      "text": "Prenatal alcohol is causally implicated in a clinical continuum — Fetal Alcohol Spectrum Disorders (FASD) — involving growth restriction, facial dysmorphology, and neurobehavioral impairment (Hoyme et al. 2016). Diagnosis requires documented exposure alongside specified diagnostic features, remaining distinct from informal personality labels.",
      "start_ratio": 0.7191,
      "end_ratio": 0.7429
    },
    {
      "id": "b-052",
      "tag": "p",
      "chunk_id": 53,
      "start_sec": 1200.649,
      "end_sec": 1231.476,
      "words": 36,
      "weight": 36,
      "text": "Maternal smoking is causally associated with lower birthweight and elevated preterm delivery risk, exhibiting clear dose-response relationships in foundational epidemiological syntheses (Kramer 1987). Cessation support remains a proven clinical and public-health priority, distinct from cognitive forecasting.",
      "start_ratio": 0.7429,
      "end_ratio": 0.762
    },
    {
      "id": "b-053",
      "tag": "p",
      "chunk_id": 54,
      "start_sec": 1231.476,
      "end_sec": 1257.107,
      "words": 43,
      "weight": 43,
      "text": "Environmental chemical mixtures represent biologically plausible exposures with limited causal identification for long-term child outcomes. While cohort studies with dense biomonitoring, such as SEPAGES, associate specific phthalate metabolites with reduced placental weight, evidence linking typical consumer-level exposures to permanent developmental injury remains weak.",
      "start_ratio": 0.762,
      "end_ratio": 0.7778
    },
    {
      "id": "b-054",
      "tag": "p",
      "chunk_id": 55,
      "start_sec": 1257.107,
      "end_sec": 1286.815,
      "words": 42,
      "weight": 42,
      "text": "Institutional chemical policy can be discussed at the system level. Household purity as a parenting identity cannot be derived from these associations. The implied actor, where there is one, is often a regulator or a product standard, not a parent performing decontamination.",
      "start_ratio": 0.7778,
      "end_ratio": 0.7962
    },
    {
      "id": "b-055",
      "tag": "h2",
      "chunk_id": 56,
      "start_sec": 1286.815,
      "end_sec": 1290.596,
      "words": 7,
      "weight": 9,
      "text": "Growth is a measurement, not a verdict",
      "start_ratio": 0.7962,
      "end_ratio": 0.7986
    },
    {
      "id": "b-056",
      "tag": "p",
      "chunk_id": 57,
      "start_sec": 1290.596,
      "end_sec": 1324.943,
      "words": 56,
      "weight": 56,
      "text": "INTERGROWTH-21st constructed international fetal-size standards from low-risk pregnancies so that a measurement can be read against a common chart rather than against a local mix of health and constraint (Papageorghiou et al. 2014). The project is prescriptive: it asks how fetuses grow when conditions are good enough, not what every fetus in every city actually does.",
      "start_ratio": 0.7986,
      "end_ratio": 0.8198
    },
    {
      "id": "b-057",
      "tag": "p",
      "chunk_id": 58,
      "start_sec": 1324.943,
      "end_sec": 1346.691,
      "words": 26,
      "weight": 26,
      "text": "Severe restriction is clinically meaningful. Dating error and constitutional smallness are not the same as placental failure. A percentile is not intelligence, worth, or future personality.",
      "start_ratio": 0.8198,
      "end_ratio": 0.8333
    },
    {
      "id": "b-058",
      "tag": "p",
      "chunk_id": 59,
      "start_sec": 1346.691,
      "end_sec": 1374.958,
      "words": 30,
      "weight": 30,
      "text": "Prenatal growth variations can attenuate, persist, or transform after birth. While international fetal-size standards provide robust clinical measurement benchmarks, newborn body size does not dictate cognitive, emotional, or social destiny.",
      "start_ratio": 0.8333,
      "end_ratio": 0.8508
    },
    {
      "id": "b-059",
      "tag": "h2",
      "chunk_id": 60,
      "start_sec": 1374.958,
      "end_sec": 1386.496,
      "words": 7,
      "weight": 9,
      "text": "What this chapter does not tell us",
      "start_ratio": 0.8508,
      "end_ratio": 0.8579
    },
    {
      "id": "b-060",
      "tag": "p",
      "chunk_id": 61,
      "start_sec": 1386.496,
      "end_sec": 1400.923,
      "words": 14,
      "weight": 14,
      "text": "It does not tell us that birth is the beginning. That is Chapter 3.",
      "start_ratio": 0.8579,
      "end_ratio": 0.8668
    },
    {
      "id": "b-061",
      "tag": "p",
      "chunk_id": 62,
      "start_sec": 1400.923,
      "end_sec": 1416.551,
      "words": 18,
      "weight": 18,
      "text": "It does not tell us how relationships organize arousal after the placenta is gone. That is Chapter 4.",
      "start_ratio": 0.8668,
      "end_ratio": 0.8765
    },
    {
      "id": "b-062",
      "tag": "p",
      "chunk_id": 63,
      "start_sec": 1416.551,
      "end_sec": 1435.378,
      "words": 22,
      "weight": 22,
      "text": "It does not tell us that a well-measured mechanism is an intervention, or that a parent’s ordinary inner life is a dose.",
      "start_ratio": 0.8765,
      "end_ratio": 0.8882
    },
    {
      "id": "b-063",
      "tag": "h2",
      "chunk_id": 64,
      "start_sec": 1435.378,
      "end_sec": 1449.075,
      "words": 3,
      "weight": 5,
      "text": "Science, Interpretation, Art",
      "start_ratio": 0.8882,
      "end_ratio": 0.8966
    },
    {
      "id": "b-064",
      "tag": "p",
      "chunk_id": 65,
      "start_sec": 1449.075,
      "end_sec": 1473.503,
      "words": 26,
      "weight": 26,
      "text": "Science here is timed process, mediated dose, unfinished construction, a few high-identification nutritional and teratologic facts, and the attenuation of popular stress forecasts under stronger designs.",
      "start_ratio": 0.8966,
      "end_ratio": 0.9117
    },
    {
      "id": "b-065",
      "tag": "p",
      "chunk_id": 66,
      "start_sec": 1473.503,
      "end_sec": 1501.61,
      "words": 49,
      "weight": 49,
      "text": "Interpretation is the habit of keeping those facts from becoming a story about the kind of person the child will be. It is also the habit of matching verbs to grades: shows where the grade is High, suggests where it is Moderate, is associated with where it is Low.",
      "start_ratio": 0.9117,
      "end_ratio": 0.9291
    },
    {
      "id": "b-066",
      "tag": "p",
      "chunk_id": 67,
      "start_sec": 1501.61,
      "end_sec": 1532.558,
      "words": 55,
      "weight": 55,
      "text": "Art is the remaining human fact: talking during pregnancy is a reasonable thing to do with another person who is already there. It does not have to be an enhancement protocol. Neither does eating, resting, or accepting help. The book’s job is to map what can be concluded, not to turn pregnancy into a performance.",
      "start_ratio": 0.9291,
      "end_ratio": 0.9483
    },
    {
      "id": "b-067",
      "tag": "h2",
      "chunk_id": 68,
      "start_sec": 1532.558,
      "end_sec": 1534.299,
      "words": 1,
      "weight": 3,
      "text": "Return",
      "start_ratio": 0.9483,
      "end_ratio": 0.9494
    },
    {
      "id": "b-068",
      "tag": "p",
      "chunk_id": 69,
      "start_sec": 1534.299,
      "end_sec": 1554.486,
      "words": 23,
      "weight": 23,
      "text": "The newborn turning toward a familiar voice has not become a different child because we now have a map. The map has changed.",
      "start_ratio": 0.9494,
      "end_ratio": 0.9619
    },
    {
      "id": "b-069",
      "tag": "p",
      "chunk_id": 70,
      "start_sec": 1554.486,
      "end_sec": 1587.954,
      "words": 53,
      "weight": 53,
      "text": "The first environment is not a sealed room. It is not a classroom. It is a succession of mediated, timed, unfinished processes: a placenta that selects, a brain still being built, a few high-identification nutritional and teratologic facts, and a much larger set of stories that do not survive contact with stronger designs.",
      "start_ratio": 0.9619,
      "end_ratio": 0.9826
    },
    {
      "id": "b-070",
      "tag": "p",
      "chunk_id": 71,
      "start_sec": 1587.954,
      "end_sec": 1616.141,
      "words": 44,
      "weight": 44,
      "text": "Birth will change the environment again. It will not start the organism from zero. Someone will have to help this child stay warm, feed, and recover from distress. That is the next territory. It is not a verdict on the pregnancy that came before.",
      "start_ratio": 0.9826,
      "end_ratio": 1.0
    }
  ]
}